Speaking at the opening of a conference of countries that have signed the Non-Proliferation Treaty (NPT) on Monday, Guterres stressed that the world is fortunate to be able to avoid the catastrophic effects of nuclear war so far. Also urged the world to continue towards the elimination of weapons of mass destruction.
But at the same time, he warned that luck is not a strategy, in the face of today's geopolitical tensions, one wrong step could lead to the catastrophic effects of nuclear war.
Currently, the total number of nuclear weapons in the world is more than 13,000, with nine countries having deadly weapons, of which the United States and Russia are reported to have more than 90 percent of the weapons. These nuclear.
Putin made the remarks in a letter to the Non-Proliferation Treaty on Monday. "Of course, no one can win a nuclear war, and such a war should not happen," the Russian strongman said in the letter. "We will support equality and inseparable security for all countries of the international community."
It should be noted that in a speech announcing the deployment of troops to invade Ukraine in late February, Putin used nuclear weapons to intimidate any country from military intervention in Ukraine. Days later, he personally ordered the Russian nuclear forces to be on high alert at all times.
