General knowledge: The eight leaders of the former Soviet Union
That same year, Lenin's health began to decline. Doctors removed a bullet from his neck that had been stuck there since the 1918 assassination attempt. However, the health of the Soviet leader deteriorated until January 21, 1924, when Lenin died of a stroke at the age of 53.
2. Joseph Stalin (1924-1953) Joseph Stalin was also an individual who participated in the October Revolution of 1917 and worked for the Soviet government under Lenin. Stalin's consolidation of power began in 1922, when he became secretary general of the Soviet Communist Party Central Committee, a position he held until his death in 1953.
On March 5, 1953, Stalin died of meningitis. Stalin's death sparked a rivalry between the Soviet leadership and, as a result, two individuals seized power at the time.
4. Nikita Cruzhev (1953-1964) Nikita Cruzchev's tenure was marked by his efforts to eradicate the evil shadows left by former Stalinist leaders and to promote international relations of the Union. Soviet.
Cruzchev lost power in 1964 when Leonid Breshev and his allies arranged for Breshev himself to become secretary general of the Communist Party. Cruz died a few years later, in 1971.
Braz Nev was the Deputy Secretary-General of the Communist Party in 1964, when he planned for the overthrow of Cruz and promoted him to Secretary-General of the Communist Party. During his tenure, Presvez pursued a "détente" policy from 1967 to 1979 that saw the easing of Cold War tensions and increased trade with the United States and its allies.
Like Andrew Pov, Chernobyl fell ill for the most part during his tenure. He died of complications from pneumonia just over a year after leading the Soviet Union.
