111 out of 131 families in dispute with private company for more than 10 years accept mediation from Botum Sakor District Authority
(Koh Kong): Botum Sakor District Authority in Koh Kong Province has opened a land distribution agreement for 131 families living in Chi Treh village, Andong Teuk commune, after more than 10 years of dispute with Sinomixin Company, resulting in 111 families. As well as accepting mediation from the authorities.
Hak Leng, the governor of Botum Sakor district, confirmed to Fresh News on the morning of August 1, 2022 that only 20 families refused to accept the Koh Kong provincial administration's explanation of the political solution. As for 111 families, they are happy to support this solution without coercion.
Botum Sakor district governor said that 20 families who refused to accept the mediation from the district authorities because they believed in the incitement of some greedy individuals want to go beyond the management policy of the leaders and some have sold Let the merchant.
Mr. Hak Leng stated that in this case, the Botum Sakor District Authority will ask for a solution from Ms. Mithona Phuthong, Governor of Koh Kong Province to 20 families who refused to accept the policy. Separately, for 111 families who accept the solution is recorded waiting for the lottery to determine the location of their lot in the future.
It should be informed that the land settled according to the win-win policy per family received 20 meters by 70 meters or 20 meters by 100 meters and 1 and a half hectares of land for farming. In particular, in the case of any citizen who sold to a trader, before the settlement process, the relevant citizen will continue to resolve on their own.
The process of allocating land to 131 families has been going on for about 10 years, but it has not been effective.
Previously, the Ministry of Environment transferred about 600 hectares of land from Sino Mixin to the Koh Kong Provincial Administration. Of that, 100 hectares will be distributed to 131 families today to end the dispute. The remaining land will be used when needed to build pagodas, schools, markets, landfills, water facilities and football fields.
