(Phnom Penh): On the morning of August 1, 2022, the General Commission of the National Police met to discuss the experience of private security management with private security service providers and enterprises (2nd time). The meeting was chaired by Lt. Gen. E Sok Khi, Deputy Commissioner of the National Police, representing General Neth Savoeun, National Police Commissioner.
Lt. Gen. Lim Sokharasmey, Director of the Department of Private Security Management, said that the meeting to gain experience in managing private security on this occasion was organized with the aim of promoting efficiency, safety, protection of property, people's lives, social security, social order and political stability. Sustainable management of private security agencies.
Lt. Gen. Lim Sokharaksmey further stated that the second meeting to gain experience in managing private security in order to:
* Second: Raise awareness of private security service providers and enterprises in the selection of private security agents about the procedures for applying for business licenses in a highly efficient and secure manner.
* Third: To manage the data, update and map a specific target of the Department of Private Security Management, private security service providers and enterprises, both legal and illegal, including private security forces and targets.
* Fourth: Encourage private security providers and enterprises to pay public service fees to the state, which is required to comply with the inter-ministerial prakas on the provision of administrative services of the Ministry of Interior to increase national budget revenue.
* Fifth: Encourage private security companies and enterprises to send all security agents who are working for companies and enterprises to receive training for private security agents to get real skills in Serve customers in accordance with national standards prepared by the Department of Private Security Management
* Sixth: Participate in sharing between the public and private sectors about the challenges in the private security sector to find more input into the drafting of the law on the management of private security for sustainable security and social order in the future.
According to Lt. Gen. Lim Sokharaksmey, there are 28,681 private security agents (2,376 females) working nationwide, including: 261 private security providers, but 233 operating companies. 14,919 private security guards (2,037 females) and 1,971 private security agency recruitment enterprises, 13,762 private security agents (339 females).
