For the April-June period, Taiwan's gross domestic product (GDP) rose 3.08 percent from a year earlier, Reuters reported, citing preliminary data released by the Taiwan Bureau of Statistics on Friday. The same as last year, compared with the 3.14% increase achieved in the first quarter.
That was lower than the 3.1 percent increase in a Reuters poll and the lowest rate since the island had a low growth rate of 0.63 percent in the second quarter of 2020, when the disease hit. The epidemic has begun to spread around the world.
Compared with the first quarter of this year, Taiwan's economy contracted by 2.93 percent year on year.
As a key hub of the global technology supply chain for large companies such as Apple Inc., Taiwan's economy outperformed its regional counterparts during the Kovid-19 pandemic, benefiting from demand. Strong for technology exports as more and more people turn to work and study at home.
But supply chain problems caused by blockades in China, its largest trading partner to control Kovid-19, as well as domestic outbreaks, have placed a strain on dependent islands. On this one trade. According to Reuters.
