Reuters reports, citing a statement issued by the World Bank, that Sri Lanka needs to adopt various structural reforms aimed at stabilizing the economy and tackling the root causes of its crisis. Foreign reserves will lead to shortages of food, fuel and medicine.
"The World Bank is deeply concerned about the dire economic situation and its impact on the people of Sri Lanka," the World Bank said.
The World Bank is redefining its target for the use of resources under existing loans to help reduce shortages of essential commodities such as medicine, gas, fertilizer for children and cash for vulnerable families.
The bank said it was working closely to establish reliable oversight and management to ensure equitable distribution.
