The disappearance of the big tiger, according to officials and conservationists, is due to trapping and hunting, wildlife trafficking, habitat loss, food loss and human disturbance.
The last big tiger was photographed in 2007 in the highlands of eastern Cambodia. Since then, there is no evidence that there is a large tiger in Cambodia, despite efforts to find it, but no trace has been found.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra said that in the past, the loss of wildlife started mostly from the use of traps, and traps are hidden killers who take the life of wildlife and are destroying the life of Cambodia's wildlife as well as wildlife. High attention of the whole world.
According to Seng Teak, Director of the Global Fund for the Conservation of Natural Resources in Cambodia (WWF), Cambodia is one of 13 countries with large tigers, and the Royal Government of Cambodia is committed to doubling the number of tigers. Large-scale global adoption of the Big Tiger Rescue Action Plan in Cambodia, which focuses on bringing big tigers into the plains of eastern Cambodia.
In addition, Mr. Seng Teak also mentioned the important benefits of the big tiger, among other benefits, the big tiger can increase the national budget revenue through tiger tourism. It can earn the highest income compared to other animals because tourists love to watch it, just one big Indian tiger can earn millions of dollars into the national economy before it loses its life.
