Mr. Chea Chanto reminded again that businesses are not allowed to take their customers' ID cards or family books as collateral for credit.
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Chea Chanto, Governor of the National Bank of Cambodia, reiterated that it is strictly forbidden to take the identity card or family book of customers as collateral. If the commercial institution continues to take the customer's identity card as collateral, it will face disciplinary action in accordance with Article 52 of the Law on Banking and Financial Institutions, such as fines.
Reminder by Mr. Chea Chanto, Governor of the National Bank of Cambodia, on July 30, 2022, on the occasion of presiding over the opening of the dissemination workshop on "Microfinance Sector in Cambodia" to stakeholders and the local public. Two in Battambang province are Kamrieng and Phnom Proek.
Mr. Chea Chanto stated, "As the monetary authority and control of the banking system, the National Bank of Cambodia strictly prohibits the retention of authentic documents of customers, such as identity cards or family books or residence books, to guarantee credit, because both authentic documents This is used for identification and statistical purposes only and has no economic value for credit guarantees or any other purpose.
He added that if any institution continues to keep the original authenticity of the customer must be returned to the customer immediately.
He said that after the country is peaceful, the country has developed in all fields, and peace and political stability have created an environment for development in all areas, including the banking and financial sectors. Well.
He claimed that the banking sector has also seen similar growth after the authorities oversaw the banking and financial system, shifting from informal borrowing to borrowing from banking and financial institutions to provide You have a real need for cash and avoid exploitation.
Mr. Chea Chanto urged all institutions to follow the principles of responsible lending in a responsible manner for the benefit of both consumers and lenders.
Mr. Kea Boran, Member of the Board of Directors of the Microfinance Association, said that as of the end of June 2022, 22 banks and microfinance institutions have been providing credit services to 12,981 people in Kamrieng district with a loan portfolio. About US $ 61 million, including $ 0.5 million in restructured loans for clients affected by Kovid 19.
