Ministry of Environment and partner organizations launch zero trap campaign in Mondulkiri province with hope for wildlife and eliminate all kinds of traps in the forest
(Mondulkiri): The Ministry of Environment cooperates with the Mondulkiri Provincial Administration with the support of the Wildlife Conservation Society (WCS) and the United States Agency for International Development (USAID), Green Heritage and other collaborative partners. Organize a zero trap campaign in Sen Monorom city, Mondulkiri province.
The ceremony was presided over by Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment and Mr. Chak Mengheang, Deputy Governor of Mondulkiri Province, with the participation of the Department of the Armed Forces, local authorities, teachers, students, local communities, people and restaurant owners. Serving tourists.
Zero Trap Campaign in Mondulkiri, 6 target provinces to stop all kinds of traps in Cambodia's protected areas in order to preserve wildlife for future generations and to eliminate all kinds of traps in protected areas All nature throughout Cambodia.
This is the fifth time that the Zero Trap Campaign has been organized out of eight campaigns, the first of which was organized by the Ministry of Environment in Phnom Penh on March 3. The second campaign in Stung Treng was organized by WWF and the third campaign was organized by WCS in Preah Vihear province, which was recently completed and the fourth in Kratie organized by Conservation International (CI).
The main objectives of the "Zero Trap Campaign" are to achieve four main objectives, including: * First: the threat posed by trapping * Second: The need for effective law enforcement Protected Areas * Third: The Importance of Reducing the Demand for Wildlife Meat * Fourth: Collaborate with provincial law enforcement authorities to combat trapping and illegal wildlife trafficking.
With the launch of the Zero Trap campaign in Mondulkiri, some restaurant owners have come out and vowed not to use wild meat to cook for their guests. In addition to the owners of these restaurants, students, residents and communities have pledged to bring their awareness of the benefits of natural resources and wildlife to their families, relatives and friends.
