A magnitude 7.1 earthquake shook the northern Philippine island of Luzon on Wednesday morning, destroying homes and buildings, including centuries-old heritage buildings and churches. Some people in the affected areas similarly said that because they were too scared to return home, they and their families decided to camp on the streets and in the park to ensure safety.
Mark Timbal, a spokesman for the Philippine National Disaster Agency, told reporters that the death toll from the quake had been reduced from five to four, but the number of injured had risen. To more than 130 people.
The Philippines is prone to natural disasters due to its active seismic position in the Pacific Ocean. Earthquakes occur frequently, and the Asean country experiences an average of 20 typhoons a year, sometimes causing landslides.
