The 10th Amendment of the Constitution, despite the stated intention to serve the national interest and the people of Cambodia, still has a number of non-governmental parties and NGOs have expressed dissatisfaction and attacked the Royal Government of Cambodia for Amend this as well.
Mr. Keut Rith, Minister of Justice, representing the Royal Government as a defender of the amendment, presented a number of arguments surrounding the amendment in terms of legal and procedural aspects.
"The 10th amendment to the constitution, as well as the previous nine amendments, was all done to protect the national interest, especially to fill the gaps in the constitution and to ensure the functioning of state institutions," he said. "The process runs smoothly, is not stuck and ensures sustainability in all circumstances."
While presenting the purpose and legitimacy of the 10th Amendment of the Constitution, Mr. Keut Rith responded to the petitions of four non-governmental political parties sent to the National Assembly requesting that the amendment not be approved. Well.
The NGO petition states that the amendment seriously affects the parliamentary regime, which has reduced the power of the National Assembly, bypassing the National Assembly in appointing the Prime Minister, giving power to political parties outside the National Assembly. Propose directly to the King to appoint an elite as Prime Minister.
Mr. Keut Rith claimed that the statements made by the four political parties were incorrect and did not fully understand the true content of the proposal for the 10th constitutional amendment and the parliamentary system of governing the country.
Mr. Keut Rith clarified a number of key points in response to the allegations made by these four non-governmental political parties. These three points include:
Therefore, the claim that the political party, which is the representative party in the National Assembly is an institution outside the National Assembly, is incorrect and does not fully examine the provisions of Article 119. However, if the political party is not elected and has no representative in the National Assembly, it should be considered as a non-parliamentary political party, as stated by the four parties that submitted the petition to the National Assembly.
3. Political parties that have submitted a petition to the National Assembly and claimed that the 10th Amendment has seriously affected the parliamentary regime should study in more depth to better understand how to form a government in the parliamentary system. In particular, this system can be studied from countries that adhere to the constitutional monarchy, which is a parliamentary regime like Cambodia. Like a constitutional monarchy in the United Kingdom or Malaysia.
Mr. Keut Rith emphasized that Cambodia's constitutional monarchy has nothing to do with any of the fundamental principles of the parliamentary system. In particular, ensuring a balance of power between the Royal Government and the National Assembly.
Keut Rith also responded to claims by the four non-governmental political parties that the amendment violated the basic principles of liberal multi-party democracy by eliminating the voices of parties with seats in the National Assembly that agreed to form a coalition government. .
Article 119 new (two) in the fourth paragraph clearly states that the appointed elite brings his colleagues who are MPs or members of the representative party in the National Assembly who are matched to hold various positions in the Royal Government to seek the trust of National Assembly. Therefore, the Royal Government will be able to have a mixed composition of the parties that are the representative parties in the Royal Government, and this case has been implemented since 1999.
