The report shows that the cash assistance program for poor and vulnerable families during the Kovid-19 war has had many positive effects, contributing to improving the lives of poor families from falling into food insecurity. , Debts, sale of family assets and eased household expenses, especially food, medicine and children's education.
The Cash Support Program for Poor and Vulnerable Families During the Fight against Kovid-19 was launched on June 24, 2020 under the chairmanship of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia. For the past 25 months, it has been supporting and facilitating the lives of more than 680,000 poor and vulnerable families with equity cards and a total budget of 744 million US dollars.
John Narith, Undersecretary of State at the Ministry of Economy and Finance and Secretary-General of the National Council for Social Protection, told reporters that the government's budget provides for the provision of cash to poor and vulnerable families during the fight against Kovid 19. This is provided transparently, that is, from the national budget to the people without going through any staff or institution.
He said that through the evaluation results show that the resources spent by the government are reaching the people, benefiting effectively and responding to the needs of the people and provide good results for the family economy and the national economy. Although the small amount of money that the people received did not help him to get out of poverty, but helped to keep the poverty from rising and falling into poverty even more.
He said that out of more than 90% of the cash received, people spend on food, which helps ensure food security at the household level and reduces malnutrition.
Sonali Dayaratne, Deputy Director of the United Nations Development Program (UNDP), said the assessment report provided "scientific evidence" as a basis for considering additional investments in social protection systems that would provide effective support for poor families. And vulnerable, affected and ensuring that no one is abandoned.
"This assessment allows us to better understand and see the impact and implications of the program on the lives of poor and vulnerable families as well as macroeconomics."
