The following is the decision to appoint a working group to deal with illegal construction and land in the Angkor resort area and other areas: Organize an orientation ceremony for 11 Cambodian officials before going to study for a master's degree in Korea. South
(Phnom Penh): The Korea International Cooperation Agency (KOICA) has organized a orientation ceremony for 11 Cambodian government officials who have received scholarships to study for a master's degree in South Korea for the 2022-2024 academic year.
With a mission to support human resource development (HRD) with partner countries, KOICA invites talented Cambodian civil servants and helps them develop the professional skills and knowledge that play an important role in Cambodia.
The Kayaka Scholarship Program is a fully funded program supported by the Government of the Republic of Korea to partner countries. The scholarship program has been in Cambodia since 1994 and aims to share Korean development experiences with Cambodia to contribute to Cambodia's long-term development needs.
To achieve this mission, KOICA has launched Master's Degree Scholarships with 20 prestigious universities in Korea in agriculture, rural development, education, governance, technology and environment and energy. For 2021, 11 outstanding officials have received master's degree scholarships with eight prestigious universities in Korea.
Scholars selected by the Scholarship Recipients thanked and pledged their support to the KOICA Office and the Government of Korea, as well as to bring about social and policy reforms in Cambodia after Learn from the Korean experience.
Korea International Cooperation Agency (KOICA) will continue to support and implement programs in Cambodia that focus on human resource development.
