EU warns Vietnamese noodles and fermenters of banned substances
The VN Express quoted a representative of the Department of Science and Technology of the Vietnamese Ministry of Industry and Trade as saying that his department was reviewing a batch of noodles in Germany. The representative said the bundles could have been exported since last year when there were no standard restrictions on ethylene oxide at the time of export.
Since February 17, the European Union has issued regulations requiring certification for packaged noodles imported into the region. These packets will be monitored for ethylene oxide levels after shipment, and so far no packets have been returned.
According to the representative, Germany said that the level of ethylene oxide in instant noodles exceeded the maximum permitted standard by two to three times. The current level of ethylene oxide in accordance with EU requirements is only 0.01 mg per kilogram.
In the future, the Department of Science and Technology of the Vietnamese Ministry of Industry and Trade will take samples of Vietnamese noodles from the Vietnamese market to assess the presence of ethylene oxide and establish standardization standards for the substance, VN Express reported.
In August last year, a variety of instant noodles made by Vietnamese companies were recalled by the European Union due to high levels of ethylene oxide. Ethylene oxide is an organic substance that combines various substances, including pesticides.
China to set up $ 44 billion fund for troubled real estate
BEIJING (Reuters) - China will launch a real estate fund to help real estate developers resolve its debt crisis by raising up to 300 billion yuan ($ 44 billion) to restore confidence in the country's central bank, officials said on Monday. Re-sector.
Reuters quoted the Chinese central bank official as saying that the move would be one of the first major steps taken by the Chinese government to rescue the real estate sector, which has been suffering from debt problems. It erupted last year.
The same source, who declined to be identified due to the sensitivity of the matter, told Reuters that the initial fund would be set at 80 billion yuan with the support of the People's Bank of China.
It added that if the fund works well, other banks will join, with the aim of increasing the size of the fund from 200 to 300 billion yuan.
One of the main pillars of China, the world's second-largest economy, is the real estate sector, but the sector has been going from crisis to crisis and has been a major driver of the country's economic growth over the years. A year ago. According to Reuters.
