(Phnom Penh): The Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts on July 25, 2022, in collaboration with the Intellectual Property Association of Cambodia (IPAC), held a workshop on "Copyright and Related Rights: Intending Protection of Intellectual Property and Dispute Resolution Mechanisms". .
The workshop was chaired by Mr. Long Bonna Sirivath, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts and Ms. Seng Vuoch Hun, President of the Cambodian Intellectual Property Association, and was attended by all stakeholders such as monks, writers, businessmen, lawyers, officials from private and public institutions. Law enforcement, arbitrators, students and many other stakeholders crowded and successfully completed.
The Secretary of State stated that in accordance with the Law on Copyright and Related Rights, as well as the content of the Bern Convention on the Protection of Literary and Artistic Works, which Cambodia has just joined on March 9, 2022. It is clear that all literary and artistic works that are created are automatically protected by the law, that is, the owner of the work is not required to register for protection.
At the same time, as a party to the Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities, the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination against Women (ICJ) That is, to protect the interests of Cambodia, which is in the early stages of enforcing copyright and similar rights on behalf of less developed countries.
Today, all dispute resolution mechanisms in cases of abuse of labor are resolved in two main ways: First, the non-judicial dispute resolution mechanism is a coordinated task with the Department of Copyright and Related Rights of the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts. As a staff member, it has been implemented in the past, and secondly, the judicial settlement mechanism in case the owner or the plaintiff can not coordinate.
Today, the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts, the founders of the Law on Copyright and Related Rights, have successfully resolved and mediated a number of disputes, including domestic and foreign disputes, under a win-win political mechanism to reduce the number of disputes. The judiciary to maintain cultural harmony under one roof of peace.
He added that the law also contributes to socio-economic development, which encourages writers, producers, artists, owners to strive to create new works and to attract investors to do business locally in accordance with national and international standards. In particular, conventions, treaties and agreements to which Cambodia is a party.
In addition, the senior official expected that this workshop really contributed to provide more knowledge, because now the business has changed, so requires traders to understand and apply. According to the principles set out in the law for the benefit of all of us in doing business.
She said the workshop provided additional insights into the content of the Investment Law, Consumer Protection Law, Competition Law and the Copyright and Related Rights Act, which are the most important laws for all investors to understand and apply. Properly manage the business, avoiding fines or penalties.
