South Korea to reduce taxes on companies, employees, workers and investors
SEOUL: South Korea on Thursday proposed a series of tax cuts for companies, employees, workers and small investors in the stock market to support private-sector-led economic growth, as President Yoon Suk-yeol said. Promises in the election campaign. According to Reuters.
In its first annual tax review, Yoon's government has proposed reducing the maximum corporate tax rate to 22% from the current 25% and lowering the income tax rate to include workers. Working to get more salary in the low income tax package at the rate of 15%.
To revive the stock market, which has fallen nearly 20 percent so far, the South Korean government has proposed a tax exemption on small investments, except for holdings worth more than 10 billion won. $ 7.63 million) in any stock.
"Our goal is to bring tax laws in line with global standards and make the business market as well as other markets more accessible," Reuters reported, quoting a statement issued by the South Korean Ministry of Finance. "Revived."
The maximum corporate tax rate of 22% in South Korea will be equal to the average rate in countries that are members of the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development (OECD), which the Ministry of Finance said will benefit "Shareholders through dividends and consumers through low prices for products and services."
The annual tax review comes as Asia's fourth-largest economy is struggling with slowing growth this year as inflation may continue to rise. According to Reuters.
