(Phnom Penh): Ms. Chou Bun Eng, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Interior and Permanent Vice Chair of the National Committee for Combating Human Trafficking (NEC), considered that the US Government's report on the Royal Government of Cambodia Black of human trafficking is a report prepared without a clear basis.
On July 19, 2022, the US State Department released its 2022 Report on Human Trafficking. In the report, the United States placed Cambodia, Vietnam, Macau, China and other countries on a watch list at Level 2 to Level 3, commonly known as blacklist, which is a bad level. The United States alleges that Cambodia and those countries made little effort and did not adhere to the standards of eliminating human trafficking.
In its 2022 report, the United States claimed that Cambodia had taken steps to continue arresting and prosecuting human traffickers and helping repatriate victims abroad, but that corruption had hampered law enforcement operations and accountability. Traders.
Regarding the ranking provided by the United States, Ms. Chou Bun Eng mentioned two main points, including:
* First, Cambodia has done a lot and has cooperated with neighboring countries and asked for coordination in repatriation in receiving victims, and cooperation in repression has been intensified. This means that Cambodia has a clear policy and plan to crack down on human trafficking.
* Second: What makes Cambodia unhappy is the promise and assertion of the US Embassy that met with Cambodian representatives that they will work closely together to help fill the gap, and the United States announces assistance to its partner organizations. To work on some projects to supplement, so that Cambodia does not fall too far or have gaps. That promise of help only made Cambodia worse off.
For Chou Bun Eng, half of ASEAN countries are blacklisted by the United States on human trafficking, including Vietnam, Malaysia, Brunei and Myanmar, while Thailand jumped to second place.
The United States has also listed China as one of the third most trafficked countries in the world, and the Chinese government has denied the allegations, saying the United States is the world's most trafficked country. Cambodia joins ICC-Angkor meeting to review 30 years of work and future direction in Paris
