The rise in consumer prices contradicts the Bank of Japan's view that the recent rise in prices in the world's third-largest economy will only be temporary. Japanese people are worried about the rising cost of living.
Nationwide core consumer price index (CPI), which excludes fresh food prices, which is volatile but includes energy, rose 2.2 percent, Reuters reported, citing data released by the government. Percentage in June compared to the same period last year.
The data, which also matched the average market forecast, meant that inflation was above the Bank of Japan's target of 2 percent for the third straight month. In May and April, the inflation rate was 2.1 percent.
So far, the Bank of Japan has not taken any action to curb rising prices, saying the rise was only an emergency due to rising global energy prices.
