(Phnom Penh): At the closing ceremony of the project to rehabilitate agro-food and tourism enterprises (aftershoot) at Raffles Royal Hotel on July 21, 2022, Mr. Kathisitha Dr. Cham Prasidh, Senior Minister, Minister of Industry, Science, Technology and Innovation encouraged To all enterprises continue to study and find out more knowledge to continue the sustainability of their business.
In addition, Senior Minister Cham Prasidh believes that support for micro, small and medium enterprises and communities in the agro-food and tourism sectors in the form of technical assistance in terms of productivity, quality and standards, support the strengthening of safety, hygiene and procedures. Minimize operations, facilities and financial assistance provided by the AFT program to address issues facing enterprises and communities.
Senior Minister Cham Prasidh said that micro and small enterprises provide a large part of the job in the Cambodian economy. Most of them are involved in industrial activities and a considerable number are struggling to continue their business. Indeed, they need ongoing support from various stakeholders to increase their competitiveness and productivity and to develop their resilience to sustain business operations.
Senior Minister Cham Prasidh said that within the framework of the Country Partnership Program, a joint program between the government and the United Nations for Industrial Development, to support the implementation of industrial development policies, he continued to support ongoing cooperation with the parties. Involve development partners to increase the productivity and competitiveness of small and medium enterprises.
He also highlighted that the agro-food and tourism rehabilitation program, called Afters, is an initiative of UNIDO Cambodia. The Afters program aims to increase the resilience and rehabilitation of micro, small and medium enterprises in the agro-food and tourism sectors in the Kovid-19 facility. This program has 3 main output elements:
First, support enterprises in the agro-food and tourism sectors to improve the quality of their products and services. Second, support enterprises in the agro-food and tourism sectors to implement safety and hygiene measures in tourism production and services. Third, support the development of national policies to promote the rehabilitation of small and medium enterprises in the agro-food and tourism sectors.
Responding to the first output, Afters has been successful in supporting enterprises to address a number of challenges through technical assistance, provision of equipment or financial assistance.
In fact, the After program provides training on digital marketing, implementation of quality and standards, compliance with hygiene and safety measures, and training on new product development, financial management, internal quality control systems and other support services to a number of enterprises. 60.
In addition, the project provides direct support, such as upgrades to equipment and tools, as well as direct cash assistance. In addition, the Afters program has supported 156 food and service businesses to enhance their expertise in hospitality, hygiene and food and beverage safety.
These skills will help ensure that these businesses can operate effectively to attract new customers, which can also benefit their business recovery in the Kovid-19 facility.
In response to the second output, Afters has launched an awareness campaign to improve safety and hygiene practices among supported enterprises and increase consumer confidence in the products and services of local micro, small and medium enterprises.
The campaign kicked off on July 4 with a series of social media campaigns, including 12 promotional videos of enterprise-sponsored products and services, as well as practical and educational videos. Safety and hygiene standards posted and attended by nearly half a million local viewers on Facebook.
In addition, the campaign also produces and distributes communication materials on improving safety and hygiene standards, as well as conducting small group activities with business owners, operators and workers in selected enterprises to promote Safety and hygiene are also educational features.
In addition, the program supported the strengthening of quality and standards for 24 agri-food enterprises and 165 businesses in compliance with standard operating procedures in the food business, as well as supporting 56 businesses to obtain certification for standard operating procedures. Re-run their business as well.
In response to the third output, the Afters program supports the integration of rehabilitation plans and elements into policies related to agriculture, food and tourism.
The Afters program also provided capacity building to 165 government officials in the agriculture, food and tourism sectors, especially in Kampong Chhnang, Pursat and Battambang provinces for the implementation of rehabilitation plans during and after the Covid-19 context.
The After program is funded by Switzerland through the Swiss Agency for Development and Cooperation (SDC) and implemented by the United Nations Industrial Development Organization (UNIDO) in partnership with the Government of Cambodia.
The main government stakeholders are the Ministry of Industry, Science, Technology and Innovation, the Ministry of Economy and Finance, the Ministry of Tourism, the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries. Other stakeholders include the private sector and associations representing the private sector or businesses in the agro-food and tourism chain.
Target areas of the program include Phnom Penh, Kampong Chhnang, Pursat and Battambang. The project has a total budget of US $ 1,000,000 with a total grant of US $ 1,475,000, including the cooperation of partners. The program started in December 2020 and will end in July 2022.
