The following is a video released by the Ministry of Tourism on the importance of minimum standard operating procedures (SOPs) in the new normal trajectory for public markets: The National Bank of Cambodia expects inflation in Cambodia to slow in the second half due to a slowdown. Fuel prices
(Phnom Penh): The Governor of the National Bank of Cambodia, Mr. Chea Chanto, expressed his hope that the inflation rate in Cambodia will decrease in the second half due to the expectation of lower oil prices.
Mr. Chea Chanto made this remark at the closing ceremony of the meeting to review the work results of the first half and the work direction of the second half of 2022 of the National Bank of Cambodia.
Inflation in Cambodia peaked at 7.2% in May 2022 as a result of rising global oil and food prices.
"Inflation is highly uncertain, mainly due to the effects of the protracted Russia-Ukraine war on oil prices and the implementation of measures to block production in China to prevent the spread of Kovid 19," he said. . "With oil prices expected to fall, inflation is projected to decline in the second half."
At the same time, the National Bank of Cambodia forecasts that the Cambodian economy will continue to grow at a rate of 5.3 percent higher than in 2021, which was mainly supported by the growth of exports of manufacturing, construction and real estate. Agriculture with the gradual recovery of tourism.
Chea Chanto stated that the International Monetary Fund has forecast that global economic growth may be lower than expected in 2022, continuing to face uncertainties and major risks, such as the effects of the Russia-Ukraine war, which have yet to find a specific solution. Yet the impact of tight monetary policy in developed countries and the new Kovid-19 virus remains a concern.
These issues have been hurting global production chains, putting pressure on global inflation and increasing capital outflows from emerging and developing countries, which will put further pressure on the value of currencies and international reserves in the country. Those.
"However, Cambodia's economic activity is expected to continue to grow at a high rate of 5.3% in 2021, with a growth rate of 3%, mainly supported by exports of manufacturing, construction and construction," he said. "And real estate and agriculture, with the gradual recovery of tourism."
The second meeting of the WHO Emergency Committee on Chickenpox will be held to examine the evidence on the worsening progression of the disease, as 14,000 cases have been reported in more than 70 countries. Spread to six more countries.
