Reuters reported, citing four sources, that the new delay was due to concerns that adverse macroeconomic conditions could affect the company's value.
Reuters quoted a CTA report as saying that the technology industry had reduced its reliance on China after imposing tariffs and turned to increasing imports from Vietnam, Taiwan, South Korea, Malaysia and other countries. Others.
About half of the $ 32 billion in tariffs comes from tariffs on Chinese-made computers and electronics, the CTA said. According to customs and border protection data, the total amount of tariffs deducted from all Chinese goods under Article 301 as of July 13 totaled $ 145.43 billion.
The report comes as Biden's government is trying to determine whether to repeal certain taxes as a way to ease the burden on US consumers from high inflation.
"With rising commodity prices across all sectors of our economy, tax abolition will reduce dangerously high inflation and reduce costs for Americans," he said.
A report by the CTA, which describes import trends since tariffs were first introduced in mid-2018, shows that China's imports of technological goods hit by tariffs under Article 301 fell 39 percent in For the past three and a half years, other goods that have not been affected by the tariff have increased by 35%.
According to the CTA, China's market share for US technology imports has been hit hard by tariffs, falling by about half to 17% in 2021 from 32% in 2017. According to Reuters.
Senior officials of the Ministry of Education advise young people to be role models and continue to educate others to achieve a tobacco-free society
(Phnom Penh): Ms. Kim Sethany, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports, continued to call on young people to apply the new knowledge they have researched and apply it to themselves and continue to set an example to encourage family, friends and other young people to stop using it. Tobacco will help prevent all forms of tobacco trafficking in order to achieve a whole society without tobacco.
Ms. Kim Sethany said this on the morning of July 21, 2022, on the occasion of presiding over the final public speaking competition under the theme: Why schools should not partner with companies Tobacco? Held at the Phnom Penh Pedagogical Institute and with the participation of Dr. Mom Kong, Executive Director of the Cambodian Movement for Health (CMH), as well as many teachers, students.
Ms. Kim Sethany said that so far, many states, including ministries and institutions have introduced a number of conventions, policies and policies to prevent tobacco, but not yet fully implemented to prevent traffic jams. It is not possible to use tobacco because there is still tobacco traffic, so if all the people answer no to tobacco, there will be no more tobacco traffic.
Ms. Kim Sethany added that the participation of young people in preventing and using all forms of tobacco is very important in conveying the message of the effects of tobacco and the harmful effects of tobacco to others for them. Stopped using tobacco, which is a complement for the youth who have helped the society as well.
At the same time, according to the young winners, they also urged all relevant institutions to implement the ban on partnerships with tobacco companies, the ban on educational institutions from cooperating with tobacco companies, in response to the national obligation to the international convention on Tobacco Control Article 5.3.
It should be noted that the main content of Article 5.3 of the International Convention is (1) the tobacco industry and public health are in conflict of fundamental interests and can not be reconciled. (2) The tobacco industry should not be encouraged to establish or operate their business because tobacco is a product that causes the death of consumers.
In addition, the outstanding youth added that the participation of the Ministry of Education, Youth and Sports is not enough and urged other ministries, such as health, economy and finance, trade, to put in place a ban policy. Collaborate with the tobacco industry as well.
