Thirteen tourism business owners receive $ 3.3 million loan from tourism rehabilitation financing project
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Lim Oun, Director General of the Small and Medium Enterprise Bank of Cambodia (SME Bank), confirmed that in the first phase of the implementation of the Royal Government's $ 150 million financing project to help restore tourism to business owners. There is a lot of interest in this area.
He said that from the date of receiving applications from July 1 to July 15, 2022, 15 business owners have applied for loans and 13 partner banks have approved the equivalent of 3.3. Million dollars.
Dr. Lim Oun said that at the beginning of the Royal Government's tourism restoration project, there were five banks that started lending first: Chip Mong Bank, CIMB Bank, Foreign Trade Bank of Cambodia, Bank Asia Pacific and ACLEDA Bank. Separately, other banks are implementing internal procedures.
He believes that this initial phase requires time to prepare the internal structure of the bank, while some banks have been working fast and started early and some banks are not yet ready in terms of guidance as well as internal procedures for implementation. Effective.
Under the Royal Government's financing project to help restore the tourism sector, $ 150 million will be provided for low-interest loans to business owners from July 1, 2022, with the participation of small and medium enterprise banks. Cambodia and 19 partner financial institutions.
According to Dr. Lim Oun, the total amount received from the 19 participating financial institutions (PFIs) totals $ 98,750,000, exceeding the actual demand of only $ 75,000,000. The current amount needed is only $ 150 million, despite the financial institutions involved.
On May 17, 2022, the Ministry of Economy and Finance announced the implementation of a co-financing project to restore and promote the growth of the tourism sector in the amount of 150 million US dollars from the Royal Government's $ 75 million through SME. Bank and financial institution contribution contributed $ 75 million.
