The Japanese Ambassador thanked Cambodia for his warm gestures and gestures for Abe's death (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): The Japanese Ambassador to Cambodia, Mikami Masahiro, expressed his heartfelt gratitude to Cambodia. And the gestures of the Cambodian leadership and people, who expressed their deepest condolences to the Japanese government and people over the death of former Prime Minister Abe Shinzo Abe.
Through the Facebook page of the Embassy of Japan in Cambodia, Mr. Mikami highlighted that on the sad occasion of the loss of former Japanese Prime Minister Abe Shinzo, many Cambodians, including members of the Royal Family, the Senate, the National Assembly, the Royal Government, institutions and the general public, attended. Pay your respects and write a message of condolence at the Embassy of Japan this week. The embassy also received countless condolences from various quarters.
"We sincerely thank all the Japanese people for their encouragement and warm gestures during this difficult time." This is a message of thanks from the Ambassador of Japan.
"While we are shocked and saddened by this tragedy, we have received real encouragement through the messages of the Cambodian people," he said.
With a message of thanks, the Ambassador also highlighted the strong ties between Cambodia and Japan. The ambassador recalled that during his two terms as prime minister, Abe paved the way for strengthening relations between Cambodia and Japan through summits and foreign policy formulation. In 2013, he also paid an official visit to Cambodia.
The Ambassador reminded that in 2013, the 60th anniversary of the establishment of diplomatic relations between Japan and Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia also visited Japan. At that time, Mr. Abe and Samdech Techo jointly pushed this bilateral relationship to reach a strategic partnership.
In this message of thanks, Mr. Mikami emphasized that the Embassy of Japan will continue its efforts to further strengthen Cambodia-Japan relations.
