The AFP news agency reported, citing U.S. government data released Wednesday, that last month's consumer price index rose higher than expected, driven by a sharp rise in gasoline prices.
The US Department of Labor reported that the 9.1 percent increase in the consumer price index in June was the highest since November 1981.
Energy contributed half of the monthly increase in prices, while gasoline rose 11.2 percent in June and 59.9 percent in the last year. Total energy prices have risen the most annually since April 1980.
The war in Ukraine has pushed up global energy and food prices, and U.S. gas prices at gas stations last month hit a record high of more than $ 5 a gallon.
However, energy prices have fallen in recent weeks, which may begin to ease some of the pressure on consumers.
But the US Federal Reserve is likely to continue its sharp rate hike as it seeks to curb rising commodity prices by curbing demand before inflation becomes more severe.
The US Federal Reserve last month raised interest rates to its highest level in nearly 30 years, and economists say it is likely to raise interest rates again by 0.75 percent this month. According to the AFP news agency.
