(Phnom Penh): Many public health experts and stakeholders believe that a higher tax increase on tobacco products will help reduce smoking and increase state revenues, while the increase is the obligation of the States Parties. Convention on Tobacco Control.
Dr. Mom Kong, Executive Director of the Cambodian Movement for Health, told Cambodia-China Friendship Radio that Cambodia is a party to the Convention on Tobacco Control, so Cambodia should raise tariffs on tobacco products in line with the guidelines. The United Nations, while many countries around the world have already raised tobacco taxes.
Dr. Mom Kong stated that raising tobacco taxes is an effective way to supplement education, put health warnings on cigarette packs, ban cigarette advertising and stop accepting subsidies under the guise of Advertising content of the tobacco industry.
"Raising tariffs on tobacco products is important because it is a win-win strategy set by the United Nations, the World Health Organization and many countries around the world," he said. The first is to increase the state tax revenue, the second is to increase the cigarette tax, to reduce the number of people who are sick, dead, disabled, to reduce the cost of the people and the government to treat diseases caused by tobacco use. Original.
"If Cambodia raises tariffs on tobacco products to 75% of the retail price of cigarettes, Cambodia will receive an additional tax revenue of approximately US $ 235 million over the next five years and US $ 933 million over the next five years," he said. The next 15 years.
The tobacco tax rate in Cambodia is only about 25% to 31% of the retail price, which is still very low compared to the rates recommended by the World Health Organization and the World Bank, and also lower than other countries. In the ASEAN region as well. ASEAN countries such as Brunei, Indonesia, Malaysia, Myanmar, Singapore, the Philippines and Thailand all have tariffs on tobacco products above 50% of retail prices.
