(Phnom Penh): With the competition between the world powers, the United States and China in the ASEAN region has become a hot topic for ASEAN, especially countries that are in conflict with China and countries that receive economic benefits from the superpowers. Both. ASEAN now serves as a regional organization to address major issues in the region as well as one of the fastest growing economies in the world.
Through the Roundtable Discussion on "ASEAN between China and the US in a New Context" organized by the Royal Academy of Cambodia this morning, July 14, 2022, Dr. Sok Touch, President of the Royal Academy of Cambodia, emphasized that ASEAN has not Hold on to any superpower, but ASEAN needs opportunities to benefit and not let ASEAN become the pitch of the world powers.
In addition, Dr. Sok Touch stated that geopolitical competition has made it difficult for Cambodia and the ASEAN region, but the ASEAN region is no great power as a big brother. ASEAN No one decides its own destiny because ASEAN has a common constitution.
The President of the Royal Academy of Cambodia continued: "ASEAN is a weakness in the context between China and the United States, so for Cambodia, which is on the path of colorful flowers, it is very right and Cambodia is not a taboo, but Cambodia. "Will be a problem solver and ASEAN is no one bigger than anyone."
According to Dr. Kin Phea, President of the Institute of International Relations of Cambodia of the Royal Academy of Cambodia, ASEAN in the midst of competition between the great powers of China and the United States is always pulling small countries or small regional organizations to their side and make ASEAN. Being at this point and so on.
Dr. Kin Phea said that for this geopolitical challenge, the United States and ASEAN could be a shield to balance its power in the Asia-Pacific region in order to prevent Chinese influence from escalating in Southeast Asia. China, on the other hand, began to expand its influence by using its soft power by pursuing win-win policies, expanding its influence by emphasizing economic development rather than political ideology.
"ASEAN is one of the most important geographical locations connecting the Indian Ocean to the Pacific Ocean and, most importantly, the China Sea, a major gateway that ASEAN members cover up to 3%," said Dr. Kin Phea. "Of the world, so through this geographical location is the main center of the two powers."
At this point, the expert on international affairs emphasized: "ASEAN has no reason to choose one superpower instead of one superpower and can not join one superpower against another superpower. . "ASEAN must avoid these problems by taking a neutral stance to ensure that ASEAN does not become a monopoly position of any great power and that ASEAN is not a pawn of that power."
