Turkey, Russia, Ukraine, UN meet to discuss grain exports
(Istanbul) - A Russian, Ukrainian and Turkish military delegation met with UN (UN) officials in Istanbul on Wednesday, July 13, to begin talks on resuming Ukrainian grain exports from seaports. Black Odesa as global food crisis worsens This is according to the release by Reuters on Wednesday, July 13, 2022.
Turkey has been working with the United Nations to negotiate an agreement following Russia's Feb. 24 invasion of Ukraine, which pushed up prices of grain, cooking oil and ghee.
Ukraine and Russia are the world's major suppliers of wheat, and Russia is also a major exporter of fertilizers, while Ukraine is also a major producer of corn and sunflower oil.
Diplomats say details of the plan being discussed include a Ukrainian-led ship carrying grain into and out of mining port waters. Russia has agreed to a ceasefire during the shipment, and Turkey, with the support of the United Nations, is inspecting ships to reduce Russia's concerns about arms smuggling.
He added that Russia wants to control and control ships to prevent "arms smuggling." The King issued a royal decree awarding medals to 65 Phnom Penh administration officials.
