(Preah Vihear): The Ministry of Environment has continued to provide training on guidelines on standard operating procedures and procedures for the investigation and preparation of criminal cases and money laundering offenses from natural resource offenses to park rangers. 40 judicial police officers and law enforcement officers of the Preah Vihear Provincial Department of Environment.
The training was held at Preah Vihear Provincial Department of Environment on the morning of July 13, 2022 under the chairmanship of Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and Permanent Deputy Chairman of the Anti-Money Laundering Task Force of the Ministry of Environment within the framework of strengthening the capacity of judicial police officers to participate. Against money laundering and terrorist financing resulting from natural resource crime.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra emphasized that in the efforts to combat money laundering and terrorist financing related to natural resource crimes, the Ministry of Environment has been actively involved with the relevant ministries and institutions of the Royal Government to strengthen the Ministry of Environment's permanent mechanisms and systems. Fight money laundering and terrorist financing and increase the effectiveness of money laundering prevention.
In this training, the Anti-Money Laundering Task Force of the Ministry of Environment made a presentation on the Law on Protected Areas, the Law on Anti-Money Laundering and Terrorist Financing, and Standard Operating Procedures for Anti-Money Laundering. .
In addition, the Secretary of State emphasized that large-scale natural resource crimes no longer exist, there are still small-scale crimes that we need to continue to prevent and crack down on.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra urged judicial police officers and law enforcement officers to pay more attention and continue to apply the law strictly and responsibly and professionally in order to increase efficiency in the protection and conservation of natural resources. And especially the prevention and suppression of crimes related to money laundering and terrorist financing related to natural resource crimes.
It should be noted that the Ministry of Environment has a total of more than 1,422 police officers and law enforcement officers.
