Days after ordering Russian troops to invade Ukraine, Putin ordered his nuclear forces to be on high alert, ready to fight at all times, and the order was seen as a warning message. That Kremlin strongman wants to tell the West, "Do not intervene militarily to help Ukraine."
The warning came true, despite US President Joe Biden providing dam-like weapons to Ukraine, but reiterated that avoiding nuclear war or World War III was a priority. One of his main administrations, with a promise not to send US troops to Ukraine, also rejected Zelensky's proposal to establish a no-fly zone in Ukraine.
In both Japan and South Korea, nuclear weapons are a complex political issue. But in the face of growing conservatism and the North Korean threat, China's growing influence, combined with a decline in US influence in the region, the two countries may find it difficult to escape the pursuit of nuclear weapons. Or weapons have a similar ability to protect their own security.
The world has long relied on the principle of mutual assurance in preventing nuclear war. But Putin's war in Ukraine is seen as destroying the Global Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty, which would impede the spread of nuclear weapons in Europe and Asia.
