Cambodia Holds Workshop on Least Developed Countries Experts on National Climate Change Adaptation Plan for Asia-Pacific
(Siem Reap): The Ministry of Environment, in collaboration with the Secretariat of the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC), has organized a workshop for experts from least developed countries on a national climate change adaptation plan for the Asia-Pacific region from the 12th. - July 15, 2022.
The workshop aims to support the Group of Least Developed Countries (LDCs) in developing and updating the National Adaptation Roadmap (NAP), sharing best practices and experiences to identify technical assistance in preparing project proposals for support. Finance from the Green Climate Fund and various climate funds.
In his opening remarks, Mr. Tin Ponlok highlighted the factors of peace, political stability and the effective action of the Royal Government of Cambodia in the management of Kovid 19, so we had the opportunity to meet in person at this important regional workshop. .
He said that Cambodia is as vulnerable to climate change as some countries in the region, and adaptation activities play an important role in reducing vulnerability to climate change and contributing to improving people's livelihoods.
At the same time, Mr. Tin Ponlok added that the main outcomes of the 26th Summit of the Parties to the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (COP26) also highlight the priorities of adaptation activities, and in particular pledges and approaches. Funds from developed countries for adaptation activities.
The second vice-president of the National Council for Sustainable Development stressed that the least developed countries need to do more to turn those funding commitments into action through technical assistance and budget through implementation. Adaptation projects.
He added that the ongoing war in Ukraine has exacerbated the vulnerabilities of least developed countries. The effects of climate change, Kovid 19 and the war have hampered efforts to achieve sustainable development goals. In addition, globalization and multilateralism are changing, which is likely to affect how countries work together to address global issues such as climate change.
"In this context of new developments, it is important for all of us to refine our development strategy towards a greener and more resilient approach to external economic or geopolitical shocks, especially in relation to food and energy security. "Cambodia has integrated 13 green and climate activities into its post-Kovid 19 development and economic recovery strategy." This is the emphasis of Mr. Tin Ponlok.
In addition, the Representative of the Secretary-General of the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (UNFCCC) expressed his gratitude to the Royal Government of Cambodia, especially the Ministry of Environment, for co-organizing this important workshop. In difficult circumstances from the spread of Kovid 19.
He continues to support Cambodia and other least developed countries in developing adaptive action plans and related techniques to help improve people's livelihoods and respond to climate change.
The four-day workshop will discuss national adaptation planning, sharing experiences in project proposal development, identifying adaptation priorities in project design to receive financial support from the Green Climate Fund, as well as other funds and Implement adaptive action plans, promote knowledge and appropriate technology for the implementation of adaptation activities in least developed countries.
The workshop highlighted the progress of action, adaptation, challenges, policies, best practices and sharing of experiences from the least developed countries in responding to climate change, especially on vulnerability reduction and improving livelihoods. The people and economy of the participating countries.
