Training of laws and regulations for 1339 tax officers who have been trained as judicial police, senior tax officials told to perform their duties well and correctly
The training lasts for three sessions, each lasting three days, with a total of 1,339 tax officials in attendance.
Dr. Ken Sambath extended a warm welcome and expressed his deep gratitude for the presence of Mr. Pen Pichsaly and the team of trainers of the Ministry of Justice who took the valuable time to participate in the opening ceremony of this training course to provide skills and knowledge about Laws and regulations related to the jurisdiction and implementation of the role of judicial police to the leaders and civil servants of the General Department of Taxation.
The Deputy Director General urges the trainees to pay more attention to understand and absorb new knowledge, skills and experience to strengthen and increase their capacity in terms of general understanding, professional skills and regulations. Ensure the efficiency and effectiveness of the work in each unit.
The training course on "Relevant Laws and Regulations for Taxpayers and Civil Servants Reached as Judicial Police" is an important subject that responds to the priority training needs of the General Department of Taxation for officers with core operational functions and Perform revenue collection work.
The purpose of this training is to promote in-depth understanding of the General Department of Taxation on this topic, as the authority to manage and collect tax revenue for the national budget must have the capacity as a judicial police to strengthen law enforcement. On effective taxation.
This training is important for tax officers who have been trained as judicial police officers and judicial police officers to investigate crimes, misdemeanors and offenses, identify and arrest perpetrators, and collect evidence that these roles and responsibilities are stipulated. And set out in the Code of Criminal Procedure.
The purpose of this course is to provide additional knowledge on the criminal field, in which we find out what a crime is and what are the elements? And in order to carry out its work as a judicial police, which institution needs more guidance and guidance? Because after being assigned the duties of a judicial police officer and a judicial police agent, the tax official must have an additional job as an assistant to the judiciary.
Dr. Ken Sambath said that this training course will provide skills to solve criminal cases and gain knowledge through in-depth study on the process of criminal cases, which is divided into three main stages, including:
(1). The investigation phase is an important lesson, especially for officers who have received this rehabilitation.
(2). The indictment phase is a phase that involves work with the prosecution, because after receiving this rehabilitation, the judiciary police have to carry out additional work under the guidance and coordination of the prosecutor.
(3). The Investigative and Trial Phase is the stage of the Investigating Judge and the Trial Judge, which is presented and explained by trainers who have experience in this field.
Dr. Ken Sambath advised the tax officials who have received this rehabilitation and should perform their work well and correctly in accordance with the prescribed legal procedures and must have close cooperation with the judiciary, which is the governing body for additional work in the relevant jurisdiction. To the implementation of this judicial police fitness.
Dr. Ken Sambath expressed his appreciation and deep gratitude to Mr. Pen Pichsaly and the team of trainers of the Ministry of Justice for their close cooperation and attention in organizing the curriculum and training. 3 more episodes.
