Brussels: The European Union (EU) finance minister on Tuesday officially confirmed that Croatia will become a member of its 20th eurozone by early 2023. This is according to the release by the AP on Tuesday, July 12, 2022.
The European Council, formed by 27 EU governments, has adopted three legal provisions to allow Croatia, an EU member state since 2013, to introduce the euro on January 1 (2023).
The European Commission also looked at the similarities between the Croatian economy and the rest of the eurozone, including the balance of payments trends, as well as verifying that Croatian law complies with EU rules governed by the EU. European Central Bank.
Croatia in southeastern Europe has been an independent country since 1991, when it left the Federation of Yugoslavia. Slovenia, a former republic of Yugoslavia and now a member of the European Union, has adopted the euro since 2007.
