Maize collectors in Phnom Proek district say that traders like to transport goods through this port because it is a regional port, so the formalities and entry and exit documents are not complicated or have to wait long. That.
According to the corn collectors, trucks transporting agricultural products through and out of the port are paid 13,000 baht or about 400 US dollars per truck to Cambodian and Thai port officials. According to the official, during the harvest season, there are dozens of vehicles passing through O'Rumduol, and some days can reach hundreds.
O'Rumduol port is not only easy to get in and out because it does not require a lot of complicated documents, but also easy to transport, that is, there is no inspection or weight limit of transport like the international level.
Trucks entering and exiting O'Romduol, especially the transport of agricultural products from Cambodia by Thai traders, are claimed to be carrying dozens of tons. There are some reports that the entry and exit through O'Rumduol also has overweight guards. Due to the overcrowding, Thai traders like to use this port to transport goods from Cambodia to Thailand.
Overcrowded transport through O'Rumduol port was reported to be a systematic collusion between port officials and a group of traders, leading to the overcrowding of Thai trucks carrying freight through the port.
There is a question as to whether the overweight transport in and out of O'Romduol by the Thai trader's car was due to the lack of skills of the checkpoint officials to inspect the overweight truck or because of the money blindfolded?
Mr. Soeum Bunrith, Deputy Governor of Battambang Province, called on border officials in Battambang Province to join in preventing this overcrowding by disseminating information related to this shipment to Thai shippers.
The Deputy Governor of Battambang Province appealed, "From now on, the heads of the border area or international border gates, which are under the jurisdiction of the provincial administration, should help educate, disseminate and distribute leaflets on overweight transport." For the Thai side to participate in the implementation. "The Thai side, as confirmed and confirmed, when they tell them, they follow us and they do not dare to violate."
By disseminating the relevant laws on transportation as well as threatening the road from overweight, it is hoped that O'Rumduol border officials will gain skills and will not allow money to blindfold overweight trucks through destruction. The road is worth millions of dollars.
