The Embassy of Japan organizes 2022 Star Festival from July 8 to 31 for the purpose of introducing the Tanabata Japanese Tradition Festival to the Cambodian people
(Phnom Penh): The Embassy of Japan in Cambodia has been organizing the 2022 Star Festival at the Cambodia-Japan Cooperation Center (CJCC) from today until July 31 for the purpose of introducing the Tanabata Japanese Tradition Festival to the people. Cambodia will further promote cultural exchanges between Japan and Cambodia.
The event was attended by Mr. Yikami Masahiro, Ambassador Extraordinary and Plenipotentiary of Japan to the Kingdom of Cambodia, Dr. Hang Chuon Naron, Minister of Education, Youth and Sports, Ms. Pen Monimara, Secretary of State and Ms. Pheung Sakona. Minister of Culture and Fine Arts and many distinguished guests.
On that occasion, Mr. Yikami Masahiro was very pleased to welcome all the guests and grandchildren at the opening ceremony of the 2022 Star Festival. Because of Kovid 19 for the past two years, the Star Festival has been held entirely online. So today I am so excited that we can meet and celebrate this festival in person.
Tanabata, or Star Festival, is a traditional Japanese festival held annually on July 7. According to legend, a couple was separated by the Amanogawa River and can only meet once a year, on July 7. On Tanabata Day, Japanese people write their wishes on colorful pieces of paper and hang them on a beautifully decorated bamboo tree.
The act of writing a wish to hang on a bamboo tree is considered to be another important new part of the prayer for the whole world to be free from Kovid-19 disease. All of our prayers have yielded satisfying results, because today we can all attend the Star Festival in person, a new page in history for the last two years.
In addition, the Minister sincerely hopes that all participants, youth and spectators will find out and learn about multiculturalism, education and new activities in line with the program of the Star Festival in 2022 with pleasure.
