BANGKOK: Those in Thailand's hotel industry have expressed concern about the government's plan to set double standards for foreign and domestic tourists, saying the changes are not feasible. According to the Bangkok Post.
"If demand increases to support hotel rates, then prices will automatically go up," Marisa said.
According to the Bangkok Post, a Thai government spokesman said on Wednesday that the hotel would be asked to apply a double standard, with foreign tourists being charged the same price as before the outbreak, while discounted rates. Prices are reserved for local guests.
"Every hotelier wants to sell at a higher and more reasonable price to make more profit, but it is difficult to do so due to fierce competition and oversupply," said Marisa. "Operators need to use pricing strategies to get cash flow."
In addition to tourism plans for international markets, she said, the government could jointly launch local campaigns with the association to provide special value for Thais and increase domestic travel.
He said the Thai government should encourage hotel operators to focus on the domestic market as rising room prices could encourage locals to travel abroad instead.
Russians rush to buy Chinese yuan to cut dollar and euro
MOSCOW (Reuters) - Russians are rushing to buy Chinese yuan to diversify their foreign currency holdings, deviating from the dollar and the euro as the finance ministry intends to start trading, Russia's state-owned bank Otkritie said. Re-exchange foreign exchange intervention in the currency of a friendly country. According to Reuters.
Demand for the yuan has risen eight-fold since May 20, when the Bank of Russia lifted restrictions on foreign currency purchases set by the bank, Otkritie said in a statement. The impact of Western sanctions on the financial sector.
Meanwhile, purchases of the British pound and the Swiss franc rose 2.5 times, while other popular currencies such as the US dollar and the euro weakened, Otkritie said.
Reuters reports, citing a new law jointly issued by China's central bank and banking regulator that banks are also banned from using the number of cards they issue or their market share as a measure of operations. Important and required to limit the number of inactive cards to 20% of the total number of cards.
"China's credit card business is growing rapidly, playing an important role in facilitating payments and consumption," the Banking and Insurance Regulatory Commission of China (CBIRC) said in a statement on its website. .
"Recently, some banks have been lax in risk management and have acted in ways that affect the interests of customers," it added.
The new law requires banks to tighten controls on credit card loans and strengthen risk management, and also set up a robust system to monitor, identify, report and prevent abuse in the card business, Reuters reported. Credit as well.
