The Royal "Tree Day" ceremony was also attended by Samdech Vibol Sena Pheakdey Say Chhum, President of the Senate, Samdech Panha Chakrei Heng Samrin, President of the National Assembly, Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of Interior, and senators of the National Assembly. The Royal Government, as well as leaders and civil servants in Battambang province.
"Plant Day" is a day that is celebrated to promote the movement of reforestation and inspire the love of the forest, natural resources in society, to participate in the care, protection and end of deforestation and natural resources. This day was established by Royal Decree No. 738 NSRKOT dated August 08, 1952 under the patronage of Samdech Norodom Sihanouk, His Majesty the King Father, His Majesty the King and Queen Norodom Monineath Sihanouk, the Queen Mother of Cambodia.
From 1992 to 2019, the "Plant Day" ceremony was held in 14 capitals and provinces across the country a total of 29 times on an area of 470 hectares 64 acres. However, due to the country facing the global crisis of Kovid 19, Cambodia has interrupted two "Plant Day" events, 2020 and 2021, to help prevent a global epidemic in the community.
Forests are very important for human and animal life on the planet, such as supporting the earth's climate, enriching the soil, bringing rainfall, as a habitat for biodiversity and the beauty of the earth. The forest is very useful on earth, it has been a weapon against many natural disasters for millions of years. Forests or trees are tools for finding food for personal use and countless others, especially the lungs of the planet.
Deforestation can lead to many consequences, such as global warming, drought, soil degradation and loss of biodiversity. Recognizing the importance of this forest, the Royal Government of Cambodia, led by Samdech Techo Hun Sen as Prime Minister, has set out a number of policies to prevent deforestation and re-establish forest cover in deforested areas.
The report on the assessment of forest cover resources in Cambodia in 2018, prepared by the National Technical Working Group and inspected by international experts and international universities, found that in 2018, forest cover resources in Cambodia, including The area of rubber plantations and oil palm plantations is 8,510,807 hectares, equivalent to 46.86 percent, and the average annual loss rate is 0.64 percent, equivalent to 115,797 hectares of the total land area nationwide.
The forest cover resources for the REDD + program, excluding rubber plantations and oil palm plantations, amounted to approximately 7,922,280 hectares, equivalent to 43.62%, with an average annual change rate of 0.71% or 129%. 810 hectares compared to the country.
The two ministries, the Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries and the Ministry of Environment, are the main staff of the Royal Government of Cambodia, which is responsible for preventing deforestation, planting and conservation of forests.
According to the report of the Ministry of Agriculture, through the Forestry Administration, the ministry has paid close attention to implementing the forest sector reform program by establishing community forests. By 2021, 643 communities will cover more than 522,000 hectares. The Ministry issued proclamations to 536 communities and approved 106 community forest management plans covering more than 72,000 hectares.
By 2021, the Ministry has established more than 499 hectares of forest extension and rehabilitation stations and received certificates of ownership of real estate on more than 101 thousand hectares. In addition, the Forestry Administration has worked closely with the National Commission and the sub-national administration to prevent and crack down on forest crime, wildlife, illegal forest land clearing, and natural resource crime.
Separately, the Ministry of Environment, which has the role of conserving forests and natural resources, has also been active in the protection of forests and inspiring the movement to love and protect forests.
