Cambodian leaders and people express deep sorrow and condolences over Abe's death
(Phnom Penh): The death of former Japanese Prime Minister Abhisit Vejjajiva due to a shooting during the parliamentary election campaign has caused great surprise and regret from the leaders of countries, politicians and citizens of various countries. In the world. In Cambodia, a country with deep ties to Japan, leaders, politicians and the people also expressed deep sorrow and grief over Abe's death.
But for Cambodia, Abe's relationship goes beyond country-to-country relations, that is, his personal relationship with the Cambodian leader, Samdech Techo Hun Sen. It is remembered that in August 2017, during his visit to Japan, Samdech Techo Hun Sen was surprised by a birthday cake prepared by Mr. Abe. That gesture shows a deep and close relationship between Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Abe.
During his tenure as Prime Minister of Japan from 2012 to 2020, Abe made significant contributions to the development of Cambodia-Japan relations. In that, he made the Cambodian relationship from a normal relationship to a strategic partnership in 2013. In addition, economic and trade relations between Cambodia and Japan continued to grow during Abe's tenure.
Starting from Abe's deep feelings for Cambodia, this has led to the leaders, politicians and the people of Cambodia deeply saddened by his death.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the head of the Royal Government of Cambodia, who has deep ties and many fond memories with Abe, expressed his shock and shock after receiving the news of Abe's death. Samdech Techo Hun Sen highlighted the video of Mr. Abe celebrating his birthday, which is a sweet and rare memory.
In his condolence message to the Prime Minister of Japan, Samdech Techo Hun Sen highlighted that Abe Shinzo is a prominent Japanese statesman who is widely recognized and praised for his competent leadership. Promoting Japan's progress and actively contributing to peace, stability and prosperity in the region and the world.
Samdech Techo further stated that Samdech and Mr. Abe Shinzo have worked closely to promote bilateral relations between Cambodia and Japan. Samdech Techo stressed that Mr. Abe Shinzo will still be highly remembered and his work will be remembered forever.
Samdech Pich Sena Tea Banh, Deputy Prime Minister and Minister of National Defense, also sent a message of condolences for Abe's death with deepest sympathy. Samdech Pich Sena Tea Banh stated, "On behalf of the officers, deputies and soldiers of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces, and on my own behalf, I would like to express my deepest condolences."
Hun Manet, Deputy Commander-in-Chief of the Royal Cambodian Armed Forces and Commander-in-Chief of the Army, who met with Abe during a visit to Japan in 2018, also expressed shock and shock at Abe's death.
On Facebook, Hun Manet expressed his sadness and shock at the news of Abe's death. Manet offered his condolences to Abe's family, as well as to the country and the people of Japan for the loss of Japan's great statesmen and leaders. He even prayed for Abe's death.
Hun Many, a young lawmaker and politician in Cambodia, also offered his condolences to the family of former Japanese Prime Minister Abe.
Hun Many considers Abe's death a great loss to the people of Japan and also the loss of a good friend of Cambodia who has contributed to the good relations between the two countries.
Dr. Keat Chhon, Honorary President of the Supreme National Economic Council, also expressed his shock and shock at the death of Abe.
In his condolence letter to the Embassy of Japan, Mr. Keat Chhon highlighted that Mr. Abe Shinzo is a longtime good friend of the Prime Minister and the Prime Minister of Cambodia, working closely to further enhance bilateral relations between Cambodia and Japan. More.
Keat Chhon said Abe Shinzo will still be remembered and his work for Cambodia will be remembered forever.
