Minister of Public Works Holds a Workshop on "Digital Government"
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Prum Sokha, Minister of Public Works, in the morning of July 7, 2022, presided over the opening ceremony of the seminar on "Digital Government" organized by the Royal School of Administration of the Ministry of Public Works of Cambodia and Singapore Institute of Public Function, co-sponsored by Temasek International Foundation.
The ceremony was held at the Ministry of Public Function and online, with the participation of the Secretary of State, Undersecretary of State, Director General of the Ministry, institutions and representatives of the capital-provincial governors, as well as the directors of all capital-provincial public functions departments, a total of more than 300 people.
At the same time, the Minister also said that leading public administration reform to build an administrative form to keep pace with the rapid evolution of modern technology in the era of Industrial Revolution 4.0, such as digital government, requires a change of mindset. Strengthen leadership and develop human resources to establish good governance structures and systems in the public and private sectors with the participation of the people.
He added that in the spirit of implementing the Royal Government of Cambodia's Digital Economy and Society Policy (2021-2035), the Ministry of Public Works is currently developing an existing information center for civil servants and salaries based on information technology. The new software section combines the use of hardware that has just received a grant from the Government of the People's Republic of China.
On that occasion, Mr. Prum Sokha expressed his strong expectation that the ladies who were present at this workshop will definitely receive new Buddhist information, including the best practices of the Industrial Revolution 4.0, such as building digital infrastructure. Capture digital and digital revolution for the benefit of future work and life.
Representatives of Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Samdech Kittipritthbandit bring candles, relics and suffixes to the monks of Wat Peung Preah Ko Preah Keo in Tbeng Meanchey district
(Preah Vihear): Mr. Heang Bunseng, Representative of Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia, and Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen, this morning, July 7, 2022, led a team to bring candles, relics and suffixes. The monk lives at Wat Peung Preah Ko Preah Keo in Moha Phal village, Chhean Mok commune, Tbeng Meanchey district, Preah Vihear province.
On that occasion, Mr. Heang Bunseng addressed the monks and informed the grandparents of the greetings from Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Samdech Kittiprittbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen, who were always thought of by both Samdech. Always pay attention to the well-being and living of all our people.
In fact, today, Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Samdech Kittipritthbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen assigned a team to bring a pair of Preah Vossa candles and many suffixes to give to the monks at Wat Peung Preah Ko Preah Keo for the Vesa Candle Festival. In the future, as well as the dedication to the souls of the great devotees, great devotees of the two Samdechs, as well as the seven relatives who have passed away to the next world.
The Chief Bishop of Wat Peung Preah Ko Preah Keo expressed his deepest gratitude to Samdech Techo Hun Sen and Samdech Kittiprittbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen for appointing a representative to bring candles and relics to the monks at this time.
The Chief Bishop of the pagoda also wished both Samdech and all his children good health to lead the Cambodian ship to be more developed to become a developed country, people with a better economy in the region. On the other hand, I would like to dedicate the merit to the great devotee, the great devotee, who is the mother and father of both Samdechs.
Dr. Kin Phea Assesses Five Points for Cambodian Diplomat's Second Visit to Myanmar
(Phnom Penh): Dr. Kin Phea, President of the Institute of International Relations of the Royal Academy of Cambodia, gave a five-point assessment on the second visit to Myanmar by Deputy Prime Minister Prak Sokhon, Minister of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation of Cambodia and Special Envoy of the ASEAN Chair. On the Myanmar issue last week.
Mr. Prak Sokhon concluded his second visit to the Republic of the Union of Myanmar from June 29 to July 3, 2022, accompanied by Mr. Ek Pap Phanthavong, Deputy Secretary-General of ASEAN in charge of the ASEAN Socio-Cultural Community. And a number of senior government officials, as well as staff of the ASEAN Secretariat.
* First, to show interest in fulfilling the role of Mr. Prak Sokhon as the Special Envoy of the ASEAN Chair for Myanmar Affairs, who has always fulfilled his responsibilities responsibly.
* Second: Demonstrate the highest will and commitment of Cambodia as the chair of ASEAN in 2022, especially Samdech Techo Hun Sen in facilitating the search for a solution to the problem in Myanmar.
* Third: It is an opportunity for Cambodia to bring the experience and win-win policy of Samdech Techo Hun Sen to Myanmar as a model in solving its problems.
During his second visit to Myanmar last weekend, Prak Sokhon was not allowed to meet with Aung San Suu Kyi and her former mistress. Clashes continue between the ruling and non-ruling groups, although the military has signed a ceasefire and once mentioned efforts to implement the ASEAN Five-Point Agreement.
On the afternoon of July 6, 2022, while briefing the Ambassador / Ambassador, members of the diplomatic corps, journalists and representatives from relevant local institutions on the results of his second working visit to Myanmar, Mr. Prak Sokhon briefed Some points include:
"The Myanmar military does not seem to have the goodwill and commitment and sincerity to implement the five points of the agreement it has signed." This is Kin Phea's statement that explains why the crisis in Myanmar is difficult to resolve.
According to Dr. Kin Pheak, the military regime led by Senior General Min Ong Lang did not allow Prak Sokhon to meet with non-government actors. He said there were three key players in the Myanmar issue that could not be ruled out.
"There are three main players in the political situation in Myanmar, the first being the military, the second being the political party that won the election or the civilian character of Myanmar, Aung San Suu Kyi," he said. The main group can not be excluded from the political solution in Myanmar and the third group is the ethnic armed group. "So a successful solution can only be achieved by involving all stakeholders in Myanmar, the so-called environmentally sound political solution."
The Special Envoy's second visit to Myanmar aims to monitor the progress of the implementation of the five-point consensus and the outcome of the ASEAN Humanitarian Assistance Consultation Meeting for Myanmar held on June 6. May 2022 in Phnom Penh and to contribute to the creation of a conducive environment for the conduct of environmental political dialogue through meetings with all stakeholders.
Prior to his second visit, Prak Sokhon sent a letter to the Council of State (SAC) of the Union of Myanmar expressing "grave concerns of ASEAN colleagues" regarding the report on the transfer of Aung San Suu Kyi from her residence to The prison in Nay Pyi Taw and urged the State Administrative Council to facilitate the repatriation of her home, where she was initially detained.
According to Kin Phea, the political solution model in Cambodia is the best example. The first is a model using the ASEAN mechanism plus the United Nations to reach a political dialogue for a common solution, and the second is a win-win policy that Cambodia successfully implements in Myanmar as well.
"A political solution is not a one-size-fits-all solution, it is a compromise, a tolerance for all, so-called a common political solution that benefits all parties," he said. "Peace, political stability and national unity and harmony are real in Myanmar."
Regarding the progress in Myanmar, Mr. Prak Sokhon asserted that the wheel of the peace process is moving forward, that is, the State Administrative Council (SAC) of Myanmar has agreed to allow individuals or groups considered to be terrorists to enter into negotiations.
