A senior official at the Ministry of Public Works dismissed allegations that the seizure of overweight trucks affected the purchase of agricultural products and claimed that the seizures were limited to overweight trucks.
(Phnom Penh): Undersecretary of State at the Ministry of Public Works and Transport, who is leading the mobile weighing team to seize the overweight truck, has denied allegations that the overweight operation was detrimental to the operation. Buy people's agricultural products. He confirmed that the seizure of overweight trucks only seize heavy overweight vehicles and vehicles not overweight by law is not caught.
In order to help prevent overloading, which causes the roads to be damaged prematurely and easily cause traffic accidents, Senior Minister Sun Chanthol, Minister of Public Works and Transport, has put in place hot measures by arresting all trucks. Overweight. Over 20% of overweight trucks will be stored for one year.
In addition to the scales, the mobile scales team also went down the national roads that do not have the scales to intercept overweight trucks and change the habit for car owners to comply with the transport law.
A recent operation to seize an overweight truck on a national road in Phnom Proek district, Battambang province, prompted some corn collectors to react, claiming that the seizure of an overweight truck prevented Thai traders from coming to transport it. Corn in Cambodia.
The Undersecretary of State confirmed that "the team came down to catch the overweight truck to protect the road. "One of our roads costs millions of dollars. If we do not help maintain our overweight as usual, then the roads do not last long, not technically we do."
Mr. Ek Rath said that in order to make the road technically usable, it should not be damaged. Preventing overweight trucks is a necessity that can not be reversed, otherwise the road is still vulnerable.
The Undersecretary of State in charge of cracking down on overcrowding crimes called on all transport operators to respect the law, do not overweight by law to participate in the maintenance of roads that the Royal Government has spent. Lots to build.
According to a report by the Ministry of Public Works and Transport, overweight shipments remain significant. In the first half of 2022, the team inspected a total of 1,831,374 vehicles. There are 1,230,010 overweight trucks and 599,721 overweight trucks.
1,643 overweight trucks were fined a total of 2.8 billion riels. Of the 1,643 vehicles fined, 220 were overweight, exceeding 20 percent of the allowable weight, and were fined and detained for one year under Article 60 of the Road Law.
The Prime Minister of Cambodia, Samdech Techo Hun Sen, has always called on and instructed the Ministry of Public Works and Transport to strictly enforce this overweight truck without exception.
