According to the Vietnamese Ministry of Industry and Trade, China's zero-concern strategy and tightening controls on the flow of goods across borders have had the greatest impact on trade.
The ministry added that the lack of logistics transportation and cross-border trade infrastructure had made the situation worse.
Vietnam's Ministry of Industry and Trade has warned that congestion at the border could escalate if no action is taken.
China was Vietnam's largest partner last year, with bilateral trade reaching $ 166 billion. Vietnam exported $ 56 billion worth of goods to China, its second-largest import market last year.
China considers selling pork from reserves to curb market gains
Authorities may sell pork from its strategic reserves in a bid to curb popular meat prices in the economic powerhouse. According to the AFP news agency.
Pork prices in the country rose sharply at the end of last month, with pork prices up 32 percent in June from a year earlier, the AFP news agency reported, citing the state-run Xinhua news agency. Last year due to declining market supply.
China's National Development and Reform Commission (NDRC) said in a statement on social media that the increase came in the wake of irregularities that hampered meat supply. Pigs and reluctance to sell live pigs into the market.
The NDRC said the reluctance to sell live pigs into the market was aimed at boosting profits and ordered major suppliers to slaughter more pigs, warning them not to stockpile them.
Most of China has survived the effects of rising world food prices caused by Russia's war in Ukraine. But pork prices have been hit hard after the country's hogs were devastated by African swine fever in recent years, which has pushed up inflation. According to the AFP news agency.
