China to set up $ 75 billion infrastructure fund to boost economy
BEIJING: China will set up a 500 billion yuan ($ 74.69 billion) state infrastructure investment fund to boost infrastructure spending and revive a declining economy. According to Reuters.
China's economy has started to recover slowly from the supply crisis caused by the general shutdown since the second quarter, although challenges to growth remain, including those in the real estate market, which continue to decline. What about declining consumer spending and fears of another wave of disease?
The fund is expected to be set up in the third quarter of this year, Reuters quoted sources as saying. This category does not provide further details.
China's Ministry of Finance and National Development and Reform Commission (NDRC) did not immediately respond to a request for comment from Reuters.
China has put in place a number of economic support measures in recent weeks, although analysts say the official 5.5 percent GDP target for this year will be difficult to achieve if the Kovid strategy is maintained at zero. Its strict.
Much of the support for the world's second-largest economy comes from fiscal stimulus to cope with the effects of Kovid-19 this year, with the central bank continuing to ease monetary policy to cut financial spending. Stuff.
Reuters quoted central bank policy adviser Wang Yiming as saying at a forum in late June that China was facing major difficulties in achieving its growth targets, and suggested that it should do so. Consider increasing the budget deficit or issuing special treasury bills.
He expects China's economy to grow by about 1 percent in the second quarter of this year from the same period last year, down sharply from 4.8 percent in the first quarter.
